## 7. Primary Key Design

### ⛔ ANTI-PATTERN: SERIAL / Auto-Increment Primary Keys

```sql
-- ❌ WRONG — creates write hotspot on last range, one node does all work
CREATE TABLE orders (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    ...
);
```

Sequential IDs route **all** inserts to the same range on the same leaseholder node. That node shows 80–90% CPU while others idle at 10–20%. You're paying for N nodes but getting 1 node's throughput.

### ✅ Correct: UUID Primary Keys

```sql
-- ✅ Writes distribute uniformly across all nodes
CREATE TABLE orders (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    customer_id UUID NOT NULL,
    amount DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL,
    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);
```

### ✅ When You Need Sequential Ordering: Hash-Sharded Indexes

```sql
-- ✅ Keeps time ordering but distributes writes across hash buckets
CREATE TABLE events (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    event_time TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
    payload JSONB,
    INDEX idx_events_time (event_time) USING HASH WITH (bucket_count = 8)
);
```

### ✅ Composite Primary Keys for Multi-Tenant

```sql
-- ✅ Well-distributed first column + natural data co-location
CREATE TABLE orders (
    tenant_id UUID NOT NULL,
    id UUID NOT NULL DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    amount DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (tenant_id, id)
);
```

